# {{senseid|en|Q1321}}{{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[Romance language]] primarily spoken in [[Spain]] and in the [[Americas]].
#: {{syn|en|Castilian}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873|author=Frederick Marryat|title=Mr. Midshipman Easy|page=163|passage="If he speaks '''Spanish''', my daughter can converse with him ; she has but shortly arrived from Spain."}}
#* {{RQ:Maugham Of Human Bondage|chapter=LXXXVI|passage=“You should read '''Spanish''',” he said. “It is a noble tongue. It has not the mellifluousness of Italian, Italian is the language of tenors and organ-grinders, but it has grandeur: it does not ripple like a brook in a garden, but it surges tumultuous like a mighty river in flood.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=w:Otto Jespersen|title=An International Language|page=48|passage=Therefore in Novial, as well as in Esp-Ido, we simplify the spelling in all words containing double letters in the national languages, from which the words are taken: ''pasa'' (E ''pass'', F ''passer''), ''efekte'', ''komun'' (F ''commun'', E ''common''), etc. In this we follow the beautiful example of '''Spanish''', which writes ''pasar'', ''efecto'', ''común'', etc., and even extend it to cases in which '''Spanish''' makes a distinction in sound and spelling, as with ''ll'' and ''rr'': ''bel'' S ''bello'', F ''belle'', ''koresponda'', S ''corresponder'', etc.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|editors=Hanna Pishwa; Karl Maroldt|title=The Development of Morphological Systematicity|page=146|passage=In contrast with the creole languages discussed above, the article systems of Rumanian, French, '''Spanish''', and Portuguese are more complex, since neutralization fails to occur to a large extent.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rami Kaminski|title=How Outsiders Can Thrive in a World That Wants Them to Fit In|magazine=Next Big Idea Club|url=https://nextbigideaclub.com/magazine/outsiders-can-thrive-world-wants-fit-bookbite/55990/|date=2025-06-19|passage=I call them otroverts—from ''otro'', the '''Spanish''' word for “other,” and ''vertere'', Latin for “to turn.” Otroverts are people who turn in a different direction: not inward like introverts, not outward like extroverts, but ''elsewhere''. They turn toward something else entirely—independence, clarity, and observation.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260823204210/https://nextbigideaclub.com/magazine/outsiders-can-thrive-world-wants-fit-bookbite/55990/|archivedate=23 August 2026}}
#* {{see more citations|en}}
